insert into public.flashcards (id, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('f5-001','core',5,'5.1',$$Policy vs standard vs procedure vs guideline?$$,$$Policy: high-level management intent (WHAT/WHY). Standard: mandatory specific requirements (e.g., AES-256). Procedure: step-by-step HOW. Guideline: recommended, not mandatory.$$),
('f5-002','core',5,'5.1',$$Name key security policies every org should have.$$,$$Acceptable use (AUP), information security, business continuity/COOP, disaster recovery, incident response, SDLC, change management.$$),
('f5-003','core',5,'5.1',$$What is an AUP?$$,$$Acceptable Use Policy - rules for appropriate use of org systems/data; signed by users; basis for enforcement.$$),
('f5-004','core',5,'5.1',$$Governance structures (exam list)?$$,$$Boards, committees, government entities; centralized vs decentralized governance models.$$),
('f5-005','core',5,'5.1',$$Data owner vs data custodian/steward vs data processor vs controller?$$,$$Owner: accountable executive who classifies data. Custodian/steward: manages/implements handling day-to-day. Controller: decides purpose/means of processing (GDPR). Processor: processes on controller's behalf.$$),
('f5-006','core',5,'5.1',$$Password standard vs access control standard vs encryption standard?$$,$$Standards make policy concrete: password (length/complexity/rotation), access control (least privilege, reviews), encryption (approved algorithms/key sizes), physical security.$$),
('f5-007','core',5,'5.1',$$External governance considerations?$$,$$Regulatory, legal, industry requirements; local/regional vs national vs global obligations - compliance is jurisdiction-dependent.$$),
('f5-008','core',5,'5.1',$$What is COOP (continuity of operations)?$$,$$Planning to keep essential functions running during/after disruption - alternate sites, procedures, personnel.$$),
('f5-009','core',5,'5.2',$$Risk identification vs risk assessment?$$,$$Identification: cataloging what could happen. Assessment: analyzing likelihood and impact to prioritize - ad hoc, recurring, one-time, or continuous.$$),
('f5-010','core',5,'5.2',$$Qualitative vs quantitative risk analysis?$$,$$Qualitative: subjective scales (high/med/low, heat maps). Quantitative: numeric monetary values (SLE, ARO, ALE) - defensible but data-hungry.$$),
('f5-011','core',5,'5.2',$$Compute: asset $200k, exposure factor 25%, expected twice per 10 years. SLE? ARO? ALE?$$,$$SLE = 200k x 0.25 = $50k. ARO = 0.2/year. ALE = 50k x 0.2 = $10k per year.$$),
('f5-012','core',5,'5.2',$$The four risk management strategies?$$,$$Avoid (stop the activity), Transfer (insurance/contracts), Mitigate (controls to reduce), Accept (documented decision to bear it - incl. exemptions/exceptions).$$),
('f5-013','core',5,'5.2',$$Risk appetite vs risk tolerance?$$,$$Appetite: overall amount/type of risk the org is WILLING to pursue (expansionary/conservative/neutral). Tolerance: acceptable deviation around specific objectives.$$),
('f5-014','core',5,'5.2',$$What is a risk register?$$,$$Living document of identified risks: description, owner, likelihood, impact, key risk indicators, treatment, status.$$),
('f5-015','core',5,'5.2',$$RTO vs RPO?$$,$$RTO: how fast service must be restored (downtime tolerance). RPO: how much data loss is tolerable, measured back in time from the incident.$$),
('f5-016','core',5,'5.2',$$MTTR vs MTBF?$$,$$MTTR: mean time to repair - average fix time. MTBF: mean time between failures - average uptime between breakdowns (reliability).$$),
('f5-017','core',5,'5.2',$$What is a BIA and what does it produce?$$,$$Business Impact Analysis: identifies critical processes, dependencies, and impact over time - produces recovery priorities and RTO/RPO targets.$$),
('f5-018','core',5,'5.3',$$Vendor assessment methods (exam list)?$$,$$Penetration testing, right-to-audit clauses, independent assessments (SOC 2, ISO), supply chain analysis, questionnaires, evidence of internal audits.$$),
('f5-019','core',5,'5.3',$$What is a right-to-audit clause?$$,$$Contract term letting you audit a vendor's security controls (or receive audit results) - verification, not trust.$$),
('f5-020','core',5,'5.3',$$Vendor selection: what is due diligence?$$,$$Investigating a vendor's security, financial, and operational health BEFORE engagement; also watch conflicts of interest.$$),
('f5-021','core',5,'5.3',$$SLA vs MOU vs MSA vs SOW vs NDA vs BPA?$$,$$SLA: measurable service commitments. MOU: non-binding intent. MSA: umbrella terms for ongoing work. SOW/WO: specific deliverables per engagement. NDA: confidentiality. BPA: business partners agreement (roles/profit).$$),
('f5-022','core',5,'5.3',$$Why does vendor monitoring continue after onboarding?$$,$$Vendor posture changes (breaches, ownership, subcontractors); ongoing monitoring, questionnaires, and rules of engagement keep third-party risk current.$$),
('f5-023','core',5,'5.4',$$Compliance reporting: internal vs external?$$,$$Internal: to leadership/board on control status. External: to regulators/auditors/customers as legally or contractually required.$$),
('f5-024','core',5,'5.4',$$Consequences of non-compliance (exam list)?$$,$$Fines, sanctions, reputational damage, loss of license, contractual impacts.$$),
('f5-025','core',5,'5.4',$$GDPR-style privacy roles and rights?$$,$$Controller decides, processor executes; data subject has rights (access, erasure/right to be forgotten); ownership and localization/sovereignty constraints apply.$$),
('f5-026','core',5,'5.4',$$What is attestation vs acknowledgement in compliance?$$,$$Attestation: formally certifying (often signing) that controls/requirements are met. Acknowledgement: confirming awareness (e.g., signing the AUP).$$),
('f5-027','core',5,'5.4',$$Privacy data: legal implications of local/national/global?$$,$$Different jurisdictions impose conflicting rules (GDPR vs state laws); where data lives and who it describes determines obligations.$$),
('f5-028','core',5,'5.5',$$Internal vs external audit vs assessment?$$,$$Audit: formal, evidence-based conformity check (internal committees or independent third party). Assessment: broader evaluation of posture, often advisory. Regulatory examinations are external.$$),
('f5-029','core',5,'5.5',$$Pen test types: physical, offensive, defensive, integrated?$$,$$Physical: test facility controls. Offensive: red team attacks. Defensive: blue team detection/response. Integrated: purple - red and blue collaborating.$$),
('f5-030','core',5,'5.5',$$Known vs partially known vs unknown environment testing?$$,$$Known (white box): full information. Partially known (gray): some info. Unknown (black box): attacker-realistic, no info - slower, less coverage.$$),
('f5-031','core',5,'5.5',$$Passive vs active reconnaissance?$$,$$Passive: gathering without touching the target (OSINT, DNS records). Active: direct interaction (scanning, probing) - detectable.$$),
('f5-032','core',5,'5.5',$$What defines pen test rules of engagement?$$,$$Scope, allowed techniques and targets, timing, communication/escalation, authorization - agreed in writing before testing.$$),
('f5-033','core',5,'5.6',$$What is security awareness training and what does it target?$$,$$Ongoing education changing user behavior: phishing recognition, password practices, social engineering, remote/hybrid work risks, policy handbooks - with reporting and monitoring of effectiveness.$$),
('f5-034','core',5,'5.6',$$What is a phishing simulation campaign?$$,$$Sending controlled fake phishing to employees to measure and improve recognition/reporting rates - training, not punishment.$$),
('f5-035','core',5,'5.6',$$Recognizing an insider threat - behaviors to watch?$$,$$Policy violations, unusual data access/downloads, working odd hours without cause, disgruntlement, bypassing controls - report through defined channels.$$),
('f5-036','core',5,'5.6',$$What is anomalous behavior recognition in awareness terms?$$,$$Training users/analysts to spot risky (accidental), unexpected, or unintentional behavior patterns and report them.$$),
('f5-037','core',5,'5.1',$$What is the SDLC policy in governance?$$,$$Rules embedding security in development: requirements, secure coding, testing gates, approvals - security shifts left.$$),
('f5-038','core',5,'5.2',$$What are Key Risk Indicators (KRIs)?$$,$$Measurable signals that risk exposure is rising (unpatched criticals count, phishing click rate) - early warning tied to the risk register.$$),
('f5-039','core',5,'5.2',$$Risk owner - who and why?$$,$$The person accountable for a risk's treatment decision - accountability cannot sit with "the security team" for business risks.$$),
('f5-040','core',5,'5.2',$$What is risk reporting?$$,$$Communicating risk posture to stakeholders (heat maps, register summaries, KRI trends) so leadership can decide on treatment and investment.$$),
('f5-041','core',5,'5.5',$$What is an examination vs attestation in audits (exam terms)?$$,$$Examination: detailed inspection of controls/evidence. Attestation: auditor formally vouches that claims meet criteria (e.g., SOC 2 report).$$),
('f5-042','core',5,'5.6',$$Operational security (OPSEC) for users?$$,$$Protecting sensitive info in daily behavior: clean desk, screen locking, not discussing sensitive matters publicly, careful social media.$$),
('f5-043','core',5,'5.6',$$Hybrid/remote work environment risks in awareness programs?$$,$$Home network security, shoulder surfing in public, personal device hygiene, phishing spikes, physical document handling.$$),
('f5-044','core',5,'5.1',$$Change management policy vs change management process?$$,$$Policy mandates that changes follow governance; the process (request, review, CAB approval, backout plan, window) implements it - covered in 1.3 too.$$),
('f5-045','core',5,'5.3',$$Supply chain analysis - what are you examining?$$,$$The vendor's own suppliers and dependencies: hardware origin, software components, service subcontractors - risk inherits transitively.$$),
('f5-046','core',5,'5.4',$$Data retention in compliance terms?$$,$$Keeping data as long as required (legal/regulatory) and NO longer - over-retention is liability; deletion schedules enforce it.$$),
('f5-047','core',5,'5.2',$$Ad hoc vs recurring vs continuous risk assessment?$$,$$Ad hoc: triggered by an event/change. Recurring: scheduled (annual). One-time: for a project. Continuous: ongoing automated evaluation.$$),
('f5-048','core',5,'5.5',$$Self-assessment vs independent third-party audit - trust difference?$$,$$Self-assessment: cheap, internal view, weakest assurance. Independent: external objectivity - required by many regulations and customers.$$),
('f5-049','core',5,'5.1',$$Playbook vs runbook (ops governance)?$$,$$Playbook: decision-oriented response plan for a scenario (phishing incident). Runbook: step-by-step operational procedure (rebuild a server). Playbooks contain runbooks.$$),
('f5-050','core',5,'5.6',$$How do you measure a security awareness program?$$,$$Phishing simulation click/report rates over time, training completion, incident reports from users, policy acknowledgements - development and execution both tracked.$$);
